# The Art of Tailoring and Sundaram
Tamil title: தையற்கலையும் சுந்தரமும்

On 8 July 1965 at the Woodlands Hotel in Madras, Perarignar Anna released *The Art of Tailoring: Children's Clothing, Part Two*, written by tailoring expert K. P. Sundaram. This compilation presents that address together with excerpts from other speeches in Madras and Kanchipuram praising the dignity and excellence of tailoring, previously published in the journal *Thaiyarkalai*.

I was delighted to learn that my respected friend K. P. Sundaram had distilled his long experience into a book. As one of those who repeatedly encouraged him to write and publish such books, I am doubly pleased.

Apart from the fact that Sundaram wrote this work while imprisoned with me, I have no connection with tailoring. I neither habitually wear many kinds of clothing nor belong to a field requiring them. I believe Sundaram invited me to release the book simply as a sign of his affection.

We feel joy and pride on learning that Sundaram is the first in Tamil Nadu to formulate tailoring methods systematically and publish them as a book. That does not mean only Sundaram should write such works. Others should too, and later authors may publish still better books. I hope his volume will guide and stimulate those that follow.

Our country generally needs many technical books. Tamils who travelled abroad were welcomed because they displayed civilisation. They must have spread their culture there and brought elements of foreign cultures home. We can therefore infer that many arts once flourished in Tamil Nadu.

The *Silappatikaram*, written about two thousand years ago, describes clothing and ornaments. Their refinement shows that ancient Tamils lived with sophistication and possessed artistic knowledge.

Every occupation in our country should have a college. Because such institutions did not exist, many crafts and arts disappeared. Others had no opportunity to learn their methods. A person's technical and artistic knowledge died with him.

Consider Siddha physicians. Others do not know the leaf used in a remedy, and the physician will not reveal it, claiming the medicine will lose its power. By concealing every technique in this way, we allowed our arts and industries to decay. We should feel not only regret but shame.

Concealing professional secrets has long been customary here. When a scorpion stings someone, a neighbour fetches a leaf secretly from behind his house, sends observers away, crushes it over the wound, and asks whether the patient is better. How can medicine develop like this?

Buy a Western medicine and its package gives its properties, ingredients and quantities, the discovering physician's name and the manufacturer's name. What clarity there, and what secrecy in our medicine!

Learning music is similar. A student may serve a master for ten or fifteen years and still struggle to learn what Kalyani raga is. Only in spare moments, when the musician is pleased, will one or two points be disclosed. Our arts were hidden and consequently failed to grow. If this continues, we cannot live like people of other countries. Sharing what one knows creates the conditions for an art to develop.

A tailor feels ashamed to let his son follow his trade. A hairdresser likewise does not wish his son to continue his. Whatever the father's occupation, he hopes the son will study and become a clerk. Do not misunderstand me: I do not say a son must practise his father's trade. Everyone should choose freely. My point is that occupations lack development and are treated as inferior, forcing people to seek others.

If each occupation offered adequate income and dignity, a weaver's son could take pride in weaving. If every occupation rose in this manner, unemployment would decline and today's poverty, scarcity and hunger would disappear.

Any so-called national dress must suit climate. A single national costume is unsuitable for an India with diverse climates and customs. A Westerner may have one outfit for work, another for sleep and another for dinner, while here people mix everything into a medley. Tamil, Telugu, Malayali, Mysorean and North Indian clothing differ; men and women wear sherwani, pyjama, coat, lungi and many other garments. How can there be one national dress? Is a stitched khadi shirt enough to make one? Clothing should suit each place and also be civilised.

Another example of concealed skill is the great musician Chittoor Nainapillai from my town. When he died, his refinements died with him.

The way nadaswaram was taught would astonish you. Four or five disciples lived in the musician's house. One cooked, another tended the cow and another ran errands. After every chore and the master's meal, he lay down and asked them to massage his legs. Immersed in that comfort, he began teaching notes and asking about relationships between ragas. Through repeated service, the disciples learned this exalted art.

Listeners can identify an Ariyakudi or Nainapillai style immediately, yet each artist's style and refinements stopped with him. If one person's discovery joined another's knowledge, new refinements could emerge.

Formal study alone is insufficient for the country's future welfare. A degree by itself does not create a livelihood, except perhaps a clerk's job in government. Modern college education is largely designed for that.

Scholars conclude that technical experience must accompany academic knowledge. Perhaps my eldest son understands this: when asked to take a B.A., he declined and chose medical training. Young people should enter technical colleges rather than merely pursue B.A. and M.A. degrees. The country needs skilled specialists.

Western countries have colleges for every field, including, I am told, tailoring. Our rulers do not recognise the need for a tailoring college, or recognise it without the clarity to act.

Occupations abroad have advanced further than ours, and public attitudes must develop with them. A worker asked to make clothing gains enthusiasm only when paid an appropriate amount, and the garment will be better. Western customers understand this. Here, ask one rupee to stitch a shirt and the customer exclaims that it is too much, goes to the next street, bargains for eight or twelve annas, and receives a shirt matching the reduced wage.

Tailoring is important not only for professional tailors but for every family. I have long associated with tailors and take pride in knowing something of the work myself. In Kanchipuram as a boy, I visited a tailor's shop in my spare time. Familiarity stirred a desire to learn, and I learned to sew buttonholes. It sometimes proved a great help to both me and my friend.

Tailors should unite in an organisation devoted to advancing their art. I welcome and praise those making the attempt. An association formed only to demand proper wages is a labour union, not an institution for developing the craft.

The needed organisation should study innovations, the respect tailors receive abroad, foreign techniques and how they can be adapted to our culture, and the relative strengths and weaknesses of Western and Indian tailoring. It should not exist merely for rights, wage increases or agitation.

B.A. and M.A. degrees should not function only as licences to apply for jobs. Education should enable discussion with teachers, exchange of sound ideas and industrial development.

Western countries have residential colleges. We have few such institutions and our rulers do not think they necessary; if pressed, they say money is unavailable. Our colleges open and close at fixed hours like factories from ten to five. They function like factories rather than places where knowledge grows.

In a true educational community, students compare lessons and books with the country's conditions, ask what each person thinks, question teachers, reflect, investigate and exchange views. The English educational terms in Images 7 and 8 are damaged by OCR, but the contrast with fixed-hour colleges is clear. I want our colleges organised on this model.

Evidence from the *Silappatikaram* period shows Tamils skilled not only in tailoring but in adornment. Two thousand years ago they sailed wooden vessels to China and the lands of the Yavanas to trade.

A shirt made from handloom cloth may not retain the expected shape. This is not the tailor's fault: even after soaking the cloth for three days and drying it before cutting, unusual starch used by weavers affects it. Cloth made by the Khadi Udyog Bhavan is rougher still and difficult even to draw through a needle.

However skilled the tailor, suitable cloth is not yet manufactured here as it is abroad. When a technical art develops, its materials must develop with it; only then can the expected result be achieved.

Tailoring needs both private effort and Government support. Government should spend at least a small sum on it as on other industries and support ready-made garment production. Just as products from Japan, America and Switzerland are sold here, our clothing should be sold abroad. Individuals lack the means to do this; Government must take the initiative. Then both tailors' livelihoods and the industry will improve.

At a time when industrial growth is proudly proclaimed, Tamil Nadu urgently needs many technical journals and books. When we demand Tamil as the medium of instruction, authorities reply that sufficient Tamil books do not exist. Publishing technical works in Tamil is essential to remove that deficiency.

Tamil technical knowledge was advanced two thousand years ago. The intervening darkness is lifting. Tamil Nadu needs many technical books and the barriers preventing skilled people from expressing their knowledge must fall. Techniques and industrial ideas should be organised by field so our industries can develop systematically.

Too many books say, “Do not do this; do not do that.” Too few say, “Do this; do that.” We need many practical books that tell people what they can accomplish, written by authors truly proficient in their fields. Sundaram has that training and mastery. Chellappan of Pari Nilayam, who printed and published the book, also deserves praise.

If instead of Sundaram we asked Karunanidhi to write it, he could: ten pages on the word “tailoring,” ten on “art,” then ten joining “tailoring” and “art.” It would be delightful reading, but it would contain no practical experience!

Dr Mu. Varadarasan explained here that Tamil technical knowledge was divided into many fields two thousand years ago. The scale may have been smaller, but evidence for many branches exists. Those in high places ask what Tamil contains and when it contained anything. Dr Mu. Va. answers that many fields existed, though not many books.

The division of knowledge into branches marks intellectual growth. The source claims Greece achieved comparable organisation only later, though the OCR corrupts the cited centuries. Whatever the chronology, the evidence of Tamil achievement establishes a distinguished past. The medieval darkness is gradually receding, and Tamils are gaining proficiency and success in many fields. This book launch is one such success.

Sundaram's mastery of tailoring is not itself surprising. What is remarkable is his wish that others gain the same mastery and his work toward that end. His public spirit deserves praise.

He completed the book while imprisoned with me during the struggle to protect Tamil. It contains both text and illustrations, with each picture properly placed beside its explanation. We have seen books where picture and explanation occur on different pages; this one is admirably arranged.

Our country has workers of commendable technical skill. Western experts who supplied specifications for manufacturing coaches at the Perambur Integral Coach Factory were paid fees. I do not know whether payments continue, but until three years ago an annual fee was sent abroad. The work happened here while money went to foreign experts.

Our workers produced a better design at lower cost than the experts' plan. I do not know what recognition it received. Why should the design not be published?

Government employees are often barred by departmental rules from publishing new ideas arising from their experience. Their capacity therefore stops at manual skill and does not benefit others. These obstacles should be removed so workers can share their intelligence.

In prison, Sundaram and Professor Anbazhagan argued about tailoring. Sundaram said ancient Tamil Nadu had no tailoring art. Anbazhagan insisted the *Silappatikaram* contained evidence. Their dispute became so intense that I had to intervene and reconcile them.

I release this book with praise and good wishes, asking Sundaram, who shows such enthusiasm for tailoring and its study, to write and publish many more works of this kind.

---NOTES---
The source is a compiled article built around Anna's 8 July 1965 Woodlands Hotel book-release address and related remarks previously printed in *Thaiyarkalai*. It is preserved as one canonical work because the website and OCR source present it that way; embedded passages are not assigned invented dates.

The Tamil OCR source is preserved verbatim. Two English educational terms in Images 7–8 and the century numerals in Image 10 are corrupted; contextual meaning is translated and exact wording is not reconstructed.

“காஜா எடுத்தல்” means stitching or working a buttonhole in tailoring, not the sweet named khaja.

The source's claims about ancient Tamil trade, Greek chronology, medical practice and foreign technical fees are translated as speech claims and require independent verification before historical citation.

The source historically refers to rough khadi material and asbestos-free garment production questions only in its own 1965 context; the translation does not turn its product assessments into present-day technical guidance.
